# Copy to api/.env for local work. Real secrets live in a secret manager,
# never in the repo (go-live-checklist.md § Security).

# Owner / migrator connection. Runs DDL. Never serves a request.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://avo:avo_dev_password@localhost:5433/avo

# Application connection. Not the table owner — this is what makes the
# REVOKE UPDATE, DELETE on audit_log an actual control.
APP_DATABASE_URL=postgres://avo_app:avo_app_dev_password@localhost:5433/avo

NODE_ENV=development

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- gateway --
# The PSP is MyFatoorah, starting with their TEST environment and test cards
# rather than waiting on live credentials.
#
# `sandbox` IS STILL THE DEFAULT, and that is a decision. e2e/ has to be
# deterministic and offline — its duplicate and out-of-order callback specs
# describe orderings no third-party network service can be asked to produce on
# cue — so the real driver is selected explicitly by whoever is exercising it.
# `sandbox` settles payments nobody made, so env.ts refuses it in production.
GATEWAY_DRIVER=sandbox

# ------------------------------------------------- MyFatoorah (test values) --
# Everything below is published in MyFatoorah's own public documentation
# (docs.myfatoorah.com/docs/api-key, /docs/test-cards). None of it is a secret and
# none of it moves real money. It is written down HERE, and deliberately NOT
# defaulted inside src/gateway/myfatoorah.ts: a credential baked into a driver as
# a fallback is how the LIVE one gets committed three weeks from now, and a driver
# that works unconfigured is a driver nobody notices is unconfigured.
#
# env.ts refuses to boot with GATEWAY_DRIVER=myfatoorah and any of the three
# missing, naming the variable.
#
#   GATEWAY_DRIVER=myfatoorah
#   MYFATOORAH_BASE_URL=https://apitest.myfatoorah.com/
#   MYFATOORAH_API_KEY=SK_KWT_vVZlnnAqu8jRByOWaRPNId4ShzEDNt256dvnjebuyzo52dXjAfRx2ixW5umjWSUx
#
# Where MyFatoorah returns the customer's BROWSER. NOT the app deep link:
# verified against the live test environment, `CallBackUrl` must be http(s) and
# `avo://topup/return?intent=…` is refused with "The field CallBackUrl must be a
# url." So this is an https URL AVO owns which forwards to the deep link, which
# the driver carries along in `to=`. Which host serves it is an infrastructure
# decision and is not invented in code.
#
#   MYFATOORAH_RETURN_URL=https://pay.avorewards.com/topups/return
#
# Their published test cards, for completing a payment on the hosted page by
# hand. There is no API call that marks a test invoice paid — every method the
# sandbox account returns has IsDirectPayment: false.
#
#   KNET          8888880000000001  exp 09/30  any 4-digit PIN  -> Captured
#                 8888880000000001  any other expiry            -> Not Captured
#   VISA/MASTER   5123450000000008  exp 01/39  CVV 100
#                 4508750015741019  any expiry / CVV
#   Cardholder    any two-part name, e.g. "test test"
#
# Live credentials are a different account and belong in a secret manager. Do not
# add them to this file even commented out.

# The key the PSP signs its callbacks with. REQUIRED in production: a webhook
# that cannot verify a signature is an unauthenticated credit endpoint. Outside
# production a missing key is generated per boot.
#
# For MyFatoorah this is the portal's "secure key" for the webhook URL. Their
# scheme is HMAC-SHA256 over an ordered `key=value` list of IDENTITY fields,
# base64, in the `myfatoorah-signature` header — with NO timestamp in the MAC and
# NOT covering `Event.Reference`, so GATEWAY_WEBHOOK_TOLERANCE_SECONDS below does
# nothing under that driver and the event-id dedupe is forgeable. The top-up state
# machine is what stops a replay crediting twice; see src/gateway/myfatoorah.ts.
# GATEWAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=at-least-sixteen-characters

# Seconds a signed callback stays acceptable — the replay window.
# GATEWAY_WEBHOOK_TOLERANCE_SECONDS=300
# Milliseconds. POST /topups calls the gateway inside its transaction, so the
# ceiling is what keeps that defensible.
# GATEWAY_TIMEOUT_MS=8000

# Where the sandbox's hosted page lives. Absolute — TopUpIntent.redirectUrl is
# a URL, not a path.
# PUBLIC_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000
# api-contract.md § TopUpIntent: where the gateway returns the customer.
# TOPUP_RETURN_URL=avo://topup/return

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ signup --
# Bounding an unauthenticated argon2id endpoint. Two tiers on the caller's
# address, the shape services/memberSearch.ts uses for the directory read.
# Generous on purpose: Kuwaiti mobile networks are heavily NAT'd and a salon
# signing walk-ins up on its own wifi is one address too, so a tight limit refuses
# real people at a counter. 100 hashes an hour is ~10 seconds of CPU.
# SIGNUP_ATTEMPTS_PER_WINDOW=20
# SIGNUP_WINDOW_MINUTES=5
# SIGNUP_ATTEMPTS_PER_HOUR=100

# WHETHER `req.ip` IS THE CUSTOMER OR THE LOAD BALANCER — and therefore whether
# the limiter above is per-caller at all. NEEDED BEFORE GO-LIVE, and only whoever
# owns the deployment can fill it in:
#
#   unset (default)  X-Forwarded-For is ignored. Behind a proxy EVERY caller
#                    shares one bucket, so one abuser can refuse a whole salon.
#   TRUST_PROXY=true one hop trusted. If ANYTHING but your own proxy can reach
#                    the API, a client sets its own address and walks past the
#                    limiter entirely.
#   TRUST_PROXY=10.0.0.0/8      an address, CIDR, or comma-separated list — the
#   TRUST_PROXY=2               correct answer, or a hop count.
#
#   TRUST_PROXY=false           there is genuinely no proxy - the socket address
#                               IS the client. A real answer, not the same as
#                               unset, and the right one for a container talking
#                               straight to the internet.
#
# UNSET IS REFUSED IN PRODUCTION, and it did not used to be. The API will not boot,
# naming this variable - DECISIONS.md section "Five calls made without asking",
# item 1. `req.ip` keys the signup limiter, which bounds BOTH an argon2 denial of
# service and the enumeration oracle on POST /auth/member/signup. Unset puts every
# caller in one bucket, so twenty probes from anybody lock out signup for everybody;
# `true` behind anything but your own proxy lets a client forge a fresh bucket per
# request and the bound is nil. A deployment that cannot say what its proxy is has
# not been configured.
#
# Development still defaults to off, which is the safer wrong when there is no proxy
# and no attacker.
# TRUST_PROXY=

# docker-compose overrides (all optional, defaults shown)
# POSTGRES_PORT=5433
# POSTGRES_USER=avo
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD=avo_dev_password
# POSTGRES_DB=avo
# APP_DB_PASSWORD=avo_app_dev_password

# --------------------------------------------------------------- receipts --
# Every settled payment queues a receipt_job row inside the money transaction.
# A worker drains it afterwards. Which provider it hands the row to is this.
#
# `logging` is the DEFAULT and is not a stub: it exercises the whole outbox —
# the claim, the lease, the backoff, the transient/permanent split, the audit of
# a receipt that could not be sent — against a driver that cannot fail. Every
# test run and every unconfigured environment uses it. It does not refuse to
# boot in production, deliberately: a logging receipt fabricates nothing, and
# refusing to start the whole API over a receipt capability is the wrong trade.
# src/receipts/logging.ts has the long version.
#
# WHATSAPP IS STILL BLOCKED AND NOT ON US. design/whatsapp-templates.md: the
# four templates are unapproved, variables are positional, and "approval is not
# instant". There is no template id to send against, so there is no driver.
RECEIPT_DRIVER=logging

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ email ---
#
# RECEIPT_DRIVER=email is the first driver that sends anything, and it is NOT
# the default.
#
# WHAT IT HANDLES: the email channel only. Selecting it leaves every `whatsapp`
# row QUEUED for a driver that does not exist yet — re-claimed, given back and
# never sent, writing no error and no audit. That is correct and it is quiet, so
# it is written here rather than discovered from a table.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT DECIDE: whether receipts send from AVO's domain or from
# per-salon subdomains. CLAUDE.md § Escalate, don't guess still lists that as the
# CLIENT'S and it is still open — it is what decides where SPF and DKIM records
# go, and no code in this repository can create those. So the sender is a
# variable with NO DEFAULT, and both answers are expressible in it:
#
#   RECEIPT_EMAIL_FROM_ADDRESS=receipts@avo.beauty              one domain
#   RECEIPT_EMAIL_FROM_ADDRESS=receipts@{salon}.avo.beauty      per salon
#
# `{salon}` is substituted from the salon id, lowercased. It is a MECHANISM, not
# a proposal: a salon id is not a mail identity, and a real per-salon scheme
# probably wants a `salon.mail_label` column. It exists so the cheap version of
# the client's answer costs a variable rather than a rewrite.
#
# THE DISPLAY NAME IS NOT CONFIGURABLE. It is the salon's, always —
# whatsapp-templates.md: "The salon name, not 'AVO', is the sender identity."
#
# NO VALUES HERE, AND NONE ANYWHERE ELSE IN THIS REPOSITORY. Same rule as the
# MyFatoorah and Supabase blocks: RECEIPT_EMAIL_API_KEY sends mail AS the
# verified domain, which is the whole of the company's sending reputation. It
# belongs in a secret manager; .env.* is gitignored precisely so it never lands
# in a commit. The API refuses to boot with RECEIPT_DRIVER=email and either of
# the two unset, naming whichever is missing.
#
# RECEIPT_EMAIL_FROM_ADDRESS=
# RECEIPT_EMAIL_API_KEY=
#
# Which postman. One value today, for the reason RECEIPT_DRIVER had one value
# yesterday. src/receipts/email/transport.ts argues HTTP over SMTP and states
# what that costs; src/receipts/email/resend.ts argues this vendor and states
# what reversing it costs.
# RECEIPT_EMAIL_TRANSPORT=resend
#
# The provider's endpoint, and a ceiling on one send. Both defaulted, because a
# published destination and a tuning number are neither of them credentials.
# RECEIPT_EMAIL_API_BASE_URL=https://api.resend.com
# RECEIPT_EMAIL_TIMEOUT_MS=10000
#
# A SERVERLESS DEPLOYMENT RUNS NO WORKER. src/serverless.ts starts no loops, so
# selecting a real driver there queues a receipt for every customer and drains
# none of them — silently, with no error row and no audit, because only a
# CLAIMED job can fail. Either run src/server.ts (render.yaml does) or schedule
# `pnpm run job:receipt-drain`. api/README.md § "What the absent background jobs
# cost" is the full argument.

# ----------------------------------------------------------------- images --
# Product and service photos. The FIRST image capability in this product, and
# the provider sits behind a seam for the same reason the PSP does: where the
# bytes are allowed to live is a client decision that has not been made —
# CLAUDE.md § Escalate lists data residency ("leaning Kuwait, undecided") and
# the retention schedule.
#
# `disk` is the only driver and it is the DEVELOPMENT one. Unlike
# GATEWAY_DRIVER=sandbox it does not refuse to boot — it refuses to WRITE when
# NODE_ENV=production, with a 503 naming the missing decision, and keeps serving
# whatever is already there. src/images/disk.ts carries the argument for the
# split: a sandbox gateway fabricates a payment, a local disk fabricates nothing
# and simply cannot keep what it was given.
#
# Adding a real store is one file next to src/images/disk.ts, one case in
# src/images/index.ts, and its credentials HERE with no defaults — same rule as
# the MyFatoorah block above, for the same reason.
IMAGE_DRIVER=disk

# --------------------------------------------------------------- supabase ---
#
# IMAGE_DRIVER=supabase is the durable driver, and it is NOT the default.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS: the deployed demo runs as a Vercel function, which has no
# writable filesystem, so `disk` answers 502 image_store_unavailable there and no
# product image works at all.
#
# WHAT SELECTING IT DOES NOT DECIDE: where a Kuwaiti salon's customer photographs
# are allowed to live. Data residency is still the client's open question
# (CLAUDE.md § Escalate, don't guess — "leaning Kuwait, undecided"). The demo
# project `avo-demo` sits in eu-central-1, in Germany, which is exactly what makes
# it a DEMO-ONLY configuration rather than a proposal. Pointing this driver at a
# production bucket is a separate act that needs that answer first.
# src/images/supabase.ts § WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT SETTLE is the long version.
#
# THE BUCKET MUST BE PRIVATE. Every read in this product is tenant-scoped —
# GET /v1/images/{id} resolves the row inside the caller's salon — and a public
# bucket makes that check decorative, because the object is then fetchable by URL
# with no principal at all.
#
# NO VALUES HERE, AND NONE ANYWHERE ELSE IN THIS REPOSITORY. Same rule as the
# MyFatoorah block above and for a sharper reason: SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY
# bypasses every row-level security policy in the project. It belongs in a secret
# manager; .env.* is gitignored precisely so it never lands in a commit. The API
# refuses to boot with IMAGE_DRIVER=supabase and any of the three unset, naming
# whichever is missing.
#
# SUPABASE_URL=https://<project-ref>.supabase.co
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=
# SUPABASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=
#
# A ceiling on one storage call. Defaulted, unlike the three above, because it is
# a tuning number and not a destination.
# SUPABASE_STORAGE_TIMEOUT_MS=10000

# Where `disk` keeps them. Relative paths resolve against api/, and the default
# is gitignored.
IMAGE_STORE_PATH=.image-store

# The product size ceiling, in bytes. 2 MiB. The route ALSO sets a Fastify body
# limit at twice this — that one is the DoS backstop and gives a blunt 413; this
# one is the product rule and gives a named error carrying the limit.
IMAGE_MAX_BYTES=2097152

# The longest side, in pixels. Read from the container header; nothing decodes.
IMAGE_MAX_DIMENSION=4096

# The pixel ceiling, and it is the decompression-bomb control. Bytes and
# dimensions do not bound each other: a 30 KB PNG can legally declare
# 30000x30000. This API never decodes so the bomb is inert here — but the wallet
# on a customer's phone does, and a merchant must not be able to upload a picture
# that kills the app of everyone who opens her shop.
#
# 12,000,000 = one photo off a phone's main camera. KEEP IT BELOW
# IMAGE_MAX_DIMENSION squared or this check cannot fire: at 4096 and 16777216 the
# two ceilings coincide exactly and the pixel one is dead code.
IMAGE_MAX_PIXELS=12000000

# Hours a detached image keeps its bytes before `pnpm --dir=<abs>/api run
# job:image-reap` removes them. The difference between an undo and a re-shoot.
IMAGE_DETACHED_GRACE_HOURS=24
